00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.570
But that is, we're excited about having
that event here.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:30.040
You did not come here tonight to hear from
a professor.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.460
You came to hear from an entrepreneur.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.388
And so to introduce him, I'm delighted to
welcome to the stage Luke Pena.

00:02:37.388 --> 00:02:40.043
Luke is a second year MBA student in the
joint degree

00:02:40.043 --> 00:02:43.335
program, between the School of Ed and the
School of Business.

00:02:43.335 --> 00:02:46.284
And Luke, if you could come forward.

00:02:46.284 --> 00:02:56.284
&gt;&gt; [SOUND].

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:05.720
&gt;&gt; From age eight to age 13, my home was
my classroom.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:06.900
And I was my own teacher.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.961
Now I have the privilege of standing
before

00:03:10.961 --> 00:03:13.809
you as Luke Pena, joint MBA MA Education
degree

00:03:13.809 --> 00:03:17.840
student, Co-President of the Stanford
Education Club, and proof

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.380
that non-traditional students can find the
path to success.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.850
It's my great honor and privilege to
welcome you all here tonight.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.994
And my responsibility to remind you to
please not

00:03:27.994 --> 00:03:31.992
use your cell phones and computers during
tonight's event.

00:03:31.992 --> 00:03:34.152
Back to the classroom of my youth.

00:03:34.152 --> 00:03:37.328
I was a hyperactive child with a
hyperactive mind.

00:03:37.328 --> 00:03:40.976
Traditional schools evaluated me and
determined that I

00:03:40.976 --> 00:03:46.170
would require either medication, or
special education courses.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.840
My mother disagreed.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.949
So my mother decided to homeschool me, and

00:03:49.949 --> 00:03:52.970
serving as my teacher through the fifth
grade.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:55.790
I took tests every year to make sure that
I was performing

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:59.970
at or above the level of my peer group in
traditional schools.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:03.051
When my mother decided to return to
undergraduate school

00:04:03.051 --> 00:04:05.628
to pursue her degree, I became my own
teacher.

00:04:05.628 --> 00:04:07.541
And for the next five years I relied on

00:04:07.541 --> 00:04:12.290
a variety of non-traditional education
resources to guide my learning.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:14.150
Without access to these resources, I would

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:17.010
never have found my own passion for
education.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.120
I would have never survived grade school,
and I

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:23.030
would never have found my way here to
Stanford.

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:24.557
I know that there are countless

00:04:24.557 --> 00:04:27.906
other students, traditional and
non-traditional, who require

00:04:27.906 --> 00:04:30.375
and desire and desperately need these
innovative

00:04:30.375 --> 00:04:33.270
resources to create their own education
opportunities.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.160
Khan Academy is creating these very

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.940
opportunities for students around the
globe.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.760
Along the way, Khan Academy is redefining
the very

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.490
way we think about teaching, about
learning, and about education.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.034
The organization is committed to changing
education for the

00:04:50.034 --> 00:04:54.490
better by providing a free, world-class
education anywhere, anytime.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:58.732
The Khan Academy website now provides
self-pacing

00:04:58.732 --> 00:05:02.034
software and includes over 3,000
instructional videos

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.763
on its YouTube channel, covering
everything from

00:05:04.763 --> 00:05:08.080
basic arithmetic to college-level science
and economics.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:14.670
It's the most used library of educational
videos on the web with 3.7 million

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.700
unique students per month, over 88 million

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:23.570
lessons delivered, and over 260 million
exercises completed.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:27.665
A growing number of classrooms around the
world are relying on Khan Academy to

00:05:27.665 --> 00:05:29.807
build student mastery of topics, and to

00:05:29.807 --> 00:05:33.210
create space in class for dynamic,
project-based learning.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.490
Khan Academy owes its success to its
founder Salman Khan.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.870
Khan was helping a young cousin with math
in

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:45.610
2004, communicating by phone and using an
interactive notepad.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.131
When others expressed interest, he began
posting his

00:05:48.131 --> 00:05:51.760
hand-scribbled tutorials on YouTube, and
demand took off.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.017
Before founding Khan Academy, he was the
portfolio manager at

00:05:55.017 --> 00:05:59.140
Khan Capital Management, and the Senior
Analyst at Wall Capital Management.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.642
Sal received his MBA from Harvard Business
School,

00:06:02.642 --> 00:06:05.200
where he was president of the student
body.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.084
He also holds a Masters in electrical
engineering

00:06:08.084 --> 00:06:11.449
and computer science, a BS in electrical
engineering and

00:06:11.449 --> 00:06:14.539
computer science, and a BS in mathematics
from MIT,

00:06:14.539 --> 00:06:17.370
where he was president of the class of
1998.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:21.555
While at MIT, Sal was the recipient of the
[UNKNOWN] Fellowship,

00:06:21.555 --> 00:06:26.451
which he used to develop web-based math
software for children with ADHD.

00:06:26.451 --> 00:06:29.301
Children not unlike myself.

00:06:29.301 --> 00:06:33.101
Ladies and gentlemen, please join me in
welcoming Sal Khan.

00:06:33.101 --> 00:06:37.906
&gt;&gt; [SOUND].

00:06:37.906 --> 00:06:44.448
&gt;&gt; Thank you much.

00:06:44.448 --> 00:06:45.333
This is very exciting.

00:06:45.333 --> 00:06:49.581
So, so how many of ya'll have been to the
site, or used the site and, in some way?

00:06:49.581 --> 00:06:50.176
Oh good.

00:06:50.176 --> 00:06:52.034
So this is a good, good, good audience.

00:06:52.034 --> 00:06:54.509
So I guess, how many of you have not been
to the site?

00:06:54.509 --> 00:06:58.448
Oh good, so, so I have something to show
you.

00:06:58.448 --> 00:07:00.380
So for those of you that have not been to
the site,

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:03.086
I have a little montage that shows what,
what the videos look like.

00:07:03.086 --> 00:07:04.936
But what we'll see over the course of
this, and I

00:07:04.936 --> 00:07:07.385
actually do wanna make it as much of a
conversation as possible.

00:07:07.385 --> 00:07:08.637
There's microphones and all the rest.

00:07:08.637 --> 00:07:10.573
I'm gonna talk for, I don't know, ten or
15 minutes.

00:07:10.573 --> 00:07:13.790
And then I just wanna have questions,
comments, anything else.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:17.322
But we'll see, Khan Academy is much more
than just a library of videos that

00:07:17.322 --> 00:07:19.510
initially I started creating, and now we
have

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:21.325
a few other people working on as well.

00:07:21.325 --> 00:07:22.089
But let me just show, oh yes.

00:07:22.089 --> 00:07:30.778
&gt;&gt; One moment to handle a technical issue.

00:07:30.778 --> 00:07:33.555
&gt;&gt; Alright that wasn't too bad.

