00:09:13.425 --> 00:09:16.090
But before we go in kind of the present of
what Khan

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:19.736
Academy's up to, I wanna talk a little bit
about how it happened.

00:09:19.736 --> 00:09:22.791
Cuz it's, it's, you know, I still kind of
wake up in the middle of the night

00:09:22.791 --> 00:09:25.000
and wonder about this very strange journey
that you're

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.892
kind of catching me hopefully in the
middle of.

00:09:26.892 --> 00:09:28.925
Or maybe very close to the beginning of,
hopefully.

00:09:28.925 --> 00:09:30.562
It's been good so far.

00:09:30.562 --> 00:09:36.921
As was introduced, 2004 I had just gotten
married.

00:09:36.921 --> 00:09:41.030
I was a newly minted MBA, graduated in
2003.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:42.890
Was working in Boston as an analyst at a
hedge

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.450
fund, and I had family visiting me from
New Orleans.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:48.556
Right after my wedding in New Jersey, they
came up to Boston.

00:09:48.556 --> 00:09:51.337
My 12 year old cousin, Nadia, her younger
brother,

00:09:51.337 --> 00:09:54.488
Arman, who's two years younger and then
the youngest, Ali.

00:09:54.488 --> 00:09:56.288
And I showed them all the sights in

00:09:56.288 --> 00:09:58.800
Boston, and I was very impressed with
Nadia.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.095
I hadn't seen her since I had left from,
you know, I

00:10:01.095 --> 00:10:03.611
grew up in New Orleans where, since I was
in high school.

00:10:03.611 --> 00:10:05.422
So, she was like two or three years old,
and now

00:10:05.422 --> 00:10:08.260
she's 12 years old, super smart girl, and
I kept encouraging her.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.947
I was like oh, you know, you should think
about some

00:10:10.947 --> 00:10:13.956
of these fancy schools that are in town,
and all the rest.

00:10:13.956 --> 00:10:16.856
And her mom told me, one morning before
Nadia had woken up, that this

00:10:16.856 --> 00:10:19.860
is very nice, Nadia views you an older
brother figure and all the rest.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:23.006
But, she's actually having trouble with
mathematics.

00:10:23.006 --> 00:10:25.570
And, I, I, had trouble believing this.

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:29.140
You know this, one I, we were having
conversations and she seemed

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:33.290
at least you know super, she intimidated
me among half the conversations.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:35.811
And on top with that you know, we shared a
certain amount of DNA.

00:10:35.811 --> 00:10:38.922
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:10:38.922 --> 00:10:41.431
And so, so I told [UNKNOWN] Nadia's
mother,

00:10:41.431 --> 00:10:43.830
that, you know, I find that hard to
believe.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:46.357
And when Nadia woke up, I said, well
what's going on?

00:10:46.357 --> 00:10:49.629
And she said, I took a placement exam, and
I, there's unit

00:10:49.629 --> 00:10:52.067
conversion, ounces to gallons, kilo, you

00:10:52.067 --> 00:10:55.740
know, kilometers, meters, miles, things
like that.

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:57.040
And she says, I just don't get that.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:59.700
I, you know, my brain just checks out.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:00.942
And I told her, I was like, look Nadia,

00:11:00.942 --> 00:11:02.760
I, I've had conversations with you last
few days.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.124
We even did, I remember these little brain
teasers while

00:11:05.124 --> 00:11:07.550
we were waiting for the fireworks over the
Charles River.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:10.352
And I said, all of that stuff that we've
been talking

00:11:10.352 --> 00:11:13.791
about is ten times deeper and more
conceptual than, than units.

00:11:13.791 --> 00:11:16.220
And I, I'm not saying this just as a kind
of a pep talk.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:17.393
You, you can do this.

00:11:17.393 --> 00:11:19.964
And, and, you know, I think like a lot of,
not just

00:11:19.964 --> 00:11:22.540
children frankly, people who've disengaged
with

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:24.837
some content, they appreciate the pep
talk.

00:11:24.837 --> 00:11:26.869
But they're like oh, okay, you know,
that's nice

00:11:26.869 --> 00:11:29.000
but, you know, it, it probably doesn't
apply to me.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.864
And so I said, well, let's make this
happen.

00:11:31.864 --> 00:11:32.963
You go back to New Orleans.

00:11:32.963 --> 00:11:35.250
They, they still lived in New Orleans.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:37.720
And, every day after work, I'll, we'll get
on something.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:40.143
We'll get on the conference phone and will
use

00:11:40.143 --> 00:11:43.387
Yahoo Doodle or something, and, and we'll
work together.

00:11:43.387 --> 00:11:44.406
And, and she agreed.

00:11:44.406 --> 00:11:47.767
I think she was skeptical, but she says,
oh, I have this cousin, kind

00:11:47.767 --> 00:11:51.145
of an older brother figure who wants to
spend time with me, worth a try.

00:11:51.145 --> 00:11:53.873
And long story short, you know, they went
back

00:11:53.873 --> 00:11:57.107
to New Orleans she, she, we started
working together.

00:11:57.107 --> 00:12:00.649
The first month was hard, but she
eventually did get past that.

00:12:00.649 --> 00:12:03.188
And then we started just doing random
topics, algebra here

00:12:03.188 --> 00:12:06.092
and there, and she actually became an
advanced math student.

00:12:06.092 --> 00:12:08.410
And then I, I kind of I, I, I've

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:11.759
coined the phrase I became somewhat of a
tiger cousin.

00:12:11.759 --> 00:12:14.384
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:12:14.384 --> 00:12:16.156
Perhaps better than a tiger mom.

00:12:16.156 --> 00:12:19.676
And, and I called her school and I said
she should retake the exam.

00:12:19.676 --> 00:12:21.388
You don't understand what she's
accomplished.

00:12:21.388 --> 00:12:24.014
There was some resistence on that part
and, and, and I'm

00:12:24.014 --> 00:12:26.800
like oh then, you know, her mom isn't
telling me this.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.951
It's, it's i'm telling and, and but, she
was able to take it, and

00:12:29.951 --> 00:12:33.020
she took the exam and she ended up
becoming a very advanced math student.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:35.057
She ended up taking calculus her freshman
year in high school.

00:12:35.057 --> 00:12:37.892
She ended up taking math at University of
New Orleans through

00:12:37.892 --> 00:12:41.092
most of high school, so she became this
really advanced math student.

00:12:41.092 --> 00:12:44.683
That same student who thought she couldn't
get units.

00:12:44.683 --> 00:12:46.074
But I was excited.

00:12:46.074 --> 00:12:50.731
You know, my day job, I wasn't able to
exercise some of these, these same ideas.

00:12:50.731 --> 00:12:55.857
So I, I I started tutoring her brothers,
things started to go well with them.

00:12:55.857 --> 00:12:57.881
Then I would, you know, would call up
random

00:12:57.881 --> 00:13:00.134
family members, and say, can I tutor your
kids?

00:13:00.134 --> 00:13:01.676
[LAUGH].

00:13:01.676 --> 00:13:03.051
&gt;&gt; And, there was kind of a tone like, oh,

00:13:03.051 --> 00:13:04.757
things must not be going so well at the
hedge fund.

00:13:04.757 --> 00:13:09.051
[LAUGH] Sal's, looking for a back up job.

00:13:09.051 --> 00:13:10.354
Which is kind of true, I guess.

00:13:10.354 --> 00:13:14.090
But but, but, so that's what happened.

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:19.121
And you fast forward to 2006 and I had a
cohort of about 15 or 18, yeah,

00:13:19.121 --> 00:13:22.324
about 15 to 20 family, friends and
relatives

00:13:22.324 --> 00:13:25.710
around the country that I was tutoring
after work.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:28.527
And it was interesting, I mean, but it
wasn't as good

00:13:28.527 --> 00:13:31.950
as those interactions with Nadia where it
was one on one.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.278
I started writing a little bit of

00:13:33.278 --> 00:13:35.541
kind of fairly primitive software
initially, so that

00:13:35.541 --> 00:13:39.360
I could give them example problems, I
could keep track of what they were doing.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:41.698
I mean, it's amazing when you, as soon as
you keep track of things and you

00:13:41.698 --> 00:13:43.245
see the real-time data, you start saying,
why

00:13:43.245 --> 00:13:44.739
is Ali doing problems at three in the
morning?

00:13:44.739 --> 00:13:46.675
This is, you know, irrespective of
algebra, this is an issue.

00:13:46.675 --> 00:13:49.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:50.610
&gt;&gt; But, but I started doing that.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.803
And in 2006, the firm I was working

00:13:52.803 --> 00:13:55.963
for, actually I was working at Wohl
Capital Management.

00:13:55.963 --> 00:13:58.939
It was a, a, a two person and a dog firm.

00:13:58.939 --> 00:14:00.676
the, the dog was our Chief Economist.

00:14:00.676 --> 00:14:04.082
And and Dan's wife who, Dan was my boss,

00:14:04.082 --> 00:14:07.975
his, his wife became a professor at
Stanford Law School.

00:14:07.975 --> 00:14:12.930
And so we moved the firm out to Palo Alto,
actually it was on Menlo Park.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:14.471
And and I was based out here.

00:14:14.471 --> 00:14:17.497
And in 2006 I was having dinner in San
Mateo with a buddy from,

00:14:17.497 --> 00:14:20.870
from college and ya know I was showing
this software that I was building.

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:23.756
And it gave you hints and generated
problems, and I was

00:14:23.756 --> 00:14:27.600
telling him about all these vid, actually
I wasn't making videos yet.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:30.830
I was telling him about these tutoring
sessions I was doing.

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:33.851
And, I told him my, my only frustration
is, is that

00:14:33.851 --> 00:14:37.100
now when I do these sessions, I do a
session with Sasha.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.600
I'm like, oh, I wish he was there when I
covered that

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:42.740
same material with Arman or with Nadia
last week or last year.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:44.840
A lot of times when I, when I did these
video

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:48.390
tutorials, you know, I would ask them,
whoa, do you get this?

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:49.380
Does this make sense to you?

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:51.798
And there would be kind of this, pregnant
pause and they

00:14:51.798 --> 00:14:54.068
would say, yeah, yeah, yeah, I get it, I
get it.

00:14:54.068 --> 00:14:55.902
And you know they didn't, but you didn't
want to push the

00:14:55.902 --> 00:14:57.669
issue and make them feel weird about it
and all the rest.

00:14:57.669 --> 00:14:59.096
They just didn't wanna waste my time.

00:14:59.096 --> 00:15:00.885
And you, you would have these things
happening.

00:15:00.885 --> 00:15:02.621
There was a sense that they, they had

00:15:02.621 --> 00:15:04.869
basic questions, but they were afraid to
ask.

00:15:04.869 --> 00:15:06.009
You know, they forgot how to divide

00:15:06.009 --> 00:15:07.531
decimals even though they're in ninth
grade.

00:15:07.531 --> 00:15:10.349
And they didn't want to embarrass
themselves, because I've been telling

00:15:10.349 --> 00:15:12.606
them that you're smart, and you can do all
of these things.

00:15:12.606 --> 00:15:19.830
And my buddy, he told me, well, you know,
there's this thing called YouTube.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:23.046
Why don't you make some tutorials on
YouTube and, and put 'em up there?

00:15:23.046 --> 00:15:26.530
And, you know, my, my initial reaction I,
no, no, that doesn't make any sense.

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:32.203
YouTube is for cats playing piano.

00:15:32.203 --> 00:15:33.018
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:15:33.018 --> 00:15:37.990
It's not for, for, for serious
mathematics.

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:41.653
But then I went home that weekend and I,
and I, and I got over

00:15:41.653 --> 00:15:46.521
the idea that, that it wasn't my idea,
which is hard for an MBA, sometimes.

00:15:46.521 --> 00:15:46.815
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:15:46.815 --> 00:15:51.587
[UNKNOWN] and, and, and, and I gave it a
shot and and it was funny.

00:15:51.587 --> 00:15:54.504
You can, yeah you can look at those first
videos there that, that are

00:15:54.504 --> 00:15:56.668
I mean literally November 2006, least
common

00:15:56.668 --> 00:15:58.946
multiples, some basic algebra, things like
that.

00:15:58.946 --> 00:16:00.854
And interesting things started to happen.

00:16:00.854 --> 00:16:02.718
I, I, one, I pointed my cousins and

00:16:02.718 --> 00:16:05.438
family friends these, these people I was
tutoring.

00:16:05.438 --> 00:16:08.077
I pointed them to the videos, I said what
do you think of them?

00:16:08.077 --> 00:16:10.184
And you know, I joke a lot about this if
any of ya'll

00:16:10.184 --> 00:16:13.033
saw the TED Talk, I talk about this too,
but it was absolutely true.

00:16:13.033 --> 00:16:16.292
The first feedback they gave me was that

00:16:16.292 --> 00:16:20.085
they preferred me on YouTube than in
person.

00:16:20.085 --> 00:16:24.340
[LAUGH] And I still haven't clarified how
broad of a statement that is.

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:26.844
If it's just math, math videos or, or not.

00:16:26.844 --> 00:16:31.346
but, but, but that, that meant a lot and,
and, and all of these ideas of, when the

00:16:31.346 --> 00:16:34.582
live session, being afraid to ask
questions, being afraid

00:16:34.582 --> 00:16:37.910
to admit that you might have forgotten
something before.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:40.370
Being afraid to ask this person you
respect or

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:43.220
this person who respects you, asking them
to repeat something.

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:44.430
You're afraid you're wasting them time.

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:45.700
Now, all of sudden, they had it all on
demand.

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:47.500
They can pause and repeat as much as they
want.

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:48.393
And they can watch it.

00:16:48.393 --> 00:16:50.180
They can watch it when they're ready for
it.

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:52.330
And when, when we're doing the live
sessions, there's a

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:54.523
huge value there, and we'll talk about
that in the future.

00:16:54.523 --> 00:16:58.439
We think that a lot of what were doing is
only going to enhance the live session.

00:16:58.439 --> 00:17:01.357
I was taking interest, there was this bond
that was forming, but the

00:17:01.357 --> 00:17:04.040
hard parts were, after work, some days I
wasn't on my A game.

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:06.062
I had a hard day at work or whatever.

00:17:06.062 --> 00:17:09.247
And, and many times I could imagine Nadia
the girl, the guy

00:17:09.247 --> 00:17:12.251
that she had a crush on asked some other
girl to the prom.

00:17:12.251 --> 00:17:14.331
And she was just depressed, and she's not
that

00:17:14.331 --> 00:17:17.460
interested in the least common multiple or
whatever, whatever else.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:19.700
And so, that was a pretty interesting
thing,

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.520
so I just kept making more and more
videos.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:26.170
my, my wife at this point was a medical
resident,

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:27.930
so I had a lot of time on my hands.

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:30.100
And, and the videos were out there.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:32.120
I kept doing more and more videos.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:35.070
And then, like, I guess a lot of stories
like this, I started

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:40.020
to realize that people that I was not
related to were watching the videos.

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:43.346
And you know, the initial comments were
things

00:17:43.346 --> 00:17:46.810
like, thank you, or, or this really
helped.

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:50.498
And I don't know how much time you all
have spent on YouTube, but

00:17:50.498 --> 00:17:53.163
most of the comments aren't quite
positive,

00:17:53.163 --> 00:17:56.230
if you're, or G-rated for that matter.

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:58.460
But, but so just that, thank you this
helped.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:01.947
But then started to get comments coming in
and, and, and as the traffic grew the

00:18:01.947 --> 00:18:03.768
comments got more and more, we get higher

00:18:03.768 --> 00:18:06.336
volume and we started getting really
touching comments.

00:18:06.336 --> 00:18:09.314
My children have dyslexia, my children
have ADHD and this

00:18:09.314 --> 00:18:11.681
is the only thing that is getting through
to them.

00:18:11.681 --> 00:18:15.126
I got a letter early on from a, from a kid
who was about to drop out of high school

00:18:15.126 --> 00:18:17.511
and he said, this was, these YouTube
videos were the

00:18:17.511 --> 00:18:20.013
only thing that got him to re-engage with
the content.

00:18:20.013 --> 00:18:26.198
Early on I got a, a letter from a parent,
saying that they're praying for me.

00:18:26.198 --> 00:18:29.212
And, and, and, you know, I, I was an
analyst at a hedge fund.

00:18:29.212 --> 00:18:30.831
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:18:30.831 --> 00:18:33.989
This was a this was other than my
investors,

00:18:33.989 --> 00:18:37.550
I don't think there was much prayer going
on.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:42.243
so, so, so, so I just kept going.

00:18:42.243 --> 00:18:44.426
And, and that was the other discovery that
started

00:18:44.426 --> 00:18:46.657
to happen, is that, this wasn't, you know,
when

00:18:46.657 --> 00:18:48.687
you look at an algebra textbook, and a
calculus

00:18:48.687 --> 00:18:51.216
textbook, there are these thousands pages
and all that.

00:18:51.216 --> 00:18:53.948
You immediately assume oh, there's, you
know, I can just make a dent in that.

00:18:53.948 --> 00:18:54.965
There's no active cover.

00:18:54.965 --> 00:18:58.081
But, you have like 70 or 80 videos, hey,
that's a pretty good scaffold of algebra.

00:18:58.081 --> 00:19:00.067
There's now like 600 or something on the
site.

00:19:00.067 --> 00:19:01.563
But that was a pretty good scaffold of
algebra.

00:19:01.563 --> 00:19:04.310
Do a few more, that's a pretty good
scaffold of, of calculus.

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:06.482
And you start realizing that, in a lot of
the

00:19:06.482 --> 00:19:10.035
world, people say, oh, you should focus on
platform, not content.

00:19:10.035 --> 00:19:11.400
Content doesn't scale.

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:12.820
But education content really does scale.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:15.460
If it's done once, it can be used forever.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.620
Calculus isn't changing every, every three
years, as the textbook

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.105
publishers would have you believe.

00:19:23.105 --> 00:19:23.778
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:19:23.778 --> 00:19:26.571
&gt;&gt; so, just kept going, and by 2009,

00:19:26.571 --> 00:19:29.372
frankly I had trouble focusing on my day
job.

00:19:29.372 --> 00:19:33.995
And I was working at another fund out here
in Palo Alto, Connective Capital.

00:19:33.995 --> 00:19:36.839
And so I you know that introduction, there
was

00:19:36.839 --> 00:19:39.484
a brief moment of Khan Capital, but it
never got

00:19:39.484 --> 00:19:42.262
beyond Khan's capital, so I kind of just,
which

00:19:42.262 --> 00:19:45.790
wasn't significant, by the way I'm
dressed, you can tell.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:49.277
But the, the so, so, I had trouble
focusing on

00:19:49.277 --> 00:19:51.527
my day job, and so I, I kinda had a sit

00:19:51.527 --> 00:19:55.310
down with my wife and we figured out,
well, someone's gotta

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:59.790
realize this is like the highest possible
social return on investment.

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:02.061
That just for one guy's salary or
whatever,

00:20:02.061 --> 00:20:03.972
you can educate, at that point I think

00:20:03.972 --> 00:20:05.943
we were getting, already we have on the

00:20:05.943 --> 00:20:08.244
order of about a million students per
month.

00:20:08.244 --> 00:20:10.455
Actually, no, we have several hundred
thousand students

00:20:10.455 --> 00:20:12.145
per month, but that can only grow over
time.

00:20:12.145 --> 00:20:15.059
And even if I got hit by a bus these
videos, in theory, could keep teaching.

00:20:15.059 --> 00:20:17.709
And if we could build out a platform and a
virtual school, we

00:20:17.709 --> 00:20:21.055
could reach an infinite number of students
and translate and all the rest.

00:20:21.055 --> 00:20:25.095
And so, somewhat naively, I had already
set up Khan Academy as a not for profit

00:20:25.095 --> 00:20:29.850
and I, I'm happy to answer any questions
about why, why we decided to do that.

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:32.498
But so I take the leap of faith.

00:20:32.498 --> 00:20:35.884
I, I had no experience really starting an
not for profit,

00:20:35.884 --> 00:20:38.659
even how do you raise money for, or any of
that.

00:20:38.659 --> 00:20:40.375
But, it was just this idea that someone

00:20:40.375 --> 00:20:42.673
should hopefully realize that this is
worth doing.

00:20:42.673 --> 00:20:44.860
And so like a lot of I think
entrepreneurial

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:47.366
stories for profit or not for profit, you
quit.

00:20:47.366 --> 00:20:48.826
There's always a few leads that you think,
oh

00:20:48.826 --> 00:20:50.630
those are gonna happen next week, and then
they don't.

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:54.053
And, and, and, about eight months into it,
you

00:20:54.053 --> 00:20:57.006
start getting really stressed, and, and, I
started updating my

00:20:57.006 --> 00:20:59.115
resume and, you know, seeing if the hedge
fund

00:20:59.115 --> 00:21:01.852
world would have me back after I
completely flaked out.

00:21:01.852 --> 00:21:03.941
God forbid starting a not-for-profit.

00:21:03.941 --> 00:21:07.471
And but then something interesting
happened.

00:21:07.471 --> 00:21:10.055
I had this little link on the site, a
PayPal link.

00:21:10.055 --> 00:21:12.146
College students around the world were,
you know,

00:21:12.146 --> 00:21:14.247
they were giving $10, $5, which meant a
lot.

00:21:14.247 --> 00:21:18.296
But all of a sudden a $10,000 donation
came in, over PayPal.

00:21:18.296 --> 00:21:20.176
And so, I, I checked who it was and, and
it

00:21:20.176 --> 00:21:22.180
was a, it was a woman by the name of Ann
Doerr.

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:25.090
And so I immediately emailed her back.

00:21:25.090 --> 00:21:27.642
And she was actually, I saw the address,
she was in Palo Alto.

00:21:27.642 --> 00:21:30.286
And I said, well, you know, thank you?

00:21:30.286 --> 00:21:34.560
If if, if we were a physical school, you
would now have a building named after you.

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:35.507
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:21:35.507 --> 00:21:37.361
&gt;&gt; If it's which is, and, and talking to

00:21:37.361 --> 00:21:39.430
the folks here, I hear that's a very good
deal.

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:41.860
I don't think $10,000 quite, quite cuts
it.

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:43.730
&gt;&gt; A water fountain.

00:21:43.730 --> 00:21:46.322
&gt;&gt; Maybe the podium, not even, yeah, the
water fountain.

00:21:46.322 --> 00:21:49.644
But, so, so Ann, Ann immediately emails
back and

00:21:49.644 --> 00:21:53.200
says, whoa, I'd, I'd be interested in
talking to you.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:57.299
So we literally, we met at one of the
Indian buffets on University Ave.

00:21:57.299 --> 00:22:00.977
And, she says, well, where are you going
with this, what do you wanna do?

00:22:00.977 --> 00:22:04.360
And, and, and her first question actually
was, how are you supporting yourself?

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:07.978
And, you know, in as proud a way as
possible, I said, I'm not.

00:22:07.978 --> 00:22:10.223
[LAUGH] And she kind of nodded and she

00:22:10.223 --> 00:22:13.253
said, well where are you hoping to take
this?

00:22:13.253 --> 00:22:16.056
And I had these, I had these slides, and I
said, well, we could build out

00:22:16.056 --> 00:22:17.775
the software that I started with my
cousins,

00:22:17.775 --> 00:22:19.509
and we could, we could reach more
students.

00:22:19.509 --> 00:22:21.832
We could translate it, we could have
interactivity on the site.

00:22:21.832 --> 00:22:23.094
We could do as much as possible

00:22:23.094 --> 00:22:25.370
of creating a real virtual interactive
experience.

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:26.090
And, she kind of nodded.

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:30.698
And then, you know, I got back in my car
and, and Ann actually got on her bike.

00:22:30.698 --> 00:22:33.665
She rode her bike to, to the buffet, which
I was very impressed.

00:22:33.665 --> 00:22:37.025
And, and, when I was driving it to my
driveway,

00:22:37.025 --> 00:22:39.437
I got a, I got a text message from Ann.

00:22:39.437 --> 00:22:43.038
And, and it said, you should be supporting
yourself.

00:22:43.038 --> 00:22:46.127
I just wired you $100,000.

00:22:46.127 --> 00:22:48.619
so, so, so it was a good day.

00:22:48.619 --> 00:22:49.932
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:22:49.932 --> 00:22:54.875
It was, it was almost, almost, crashed
into the garage.

00:22:54.875 --> 00:23:00.921
And as we will see in this story, Ann's
text messages are always good things.

00:23:00.921 --> 00:23:02.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:23:02.390 --> 00:23:03.869
&gt;&gt; So this wasn't, this wasn't that long
ago.

00:23:03.869 --> 00:23:06.518
This was literally about, not even two
years ago.

00:23:06.518 --> 00:23:10.689
This was May of 2010, and, right around
that same time, actually a few weeks

00:23:10.689 --> 00:23:12.926
later, but these were all kind of
unrelated

00:23:12.926 --> 00:23:15.360
events some folks at Google brought me in.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:17.053
You know, I, I say, you know, there's two

00:23:17.053 --> 00:23:19.651
major institutions in Mountain View,
Google and the Khan Academy.

00:23:19.651 --> 00:23:21.988
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:23:21.988 --> 00:23:23.661
It's they don't laugh as much.

00:23:23.661 --> 00:23:28.107
But the, the and, they brought me in and
it was kind of mysterious meeting.

00:23:28.107 --> 00:23:30.816
They said you know a lot of our children
are using your stuff.

00:23:30.816 --> 00:23:32.520
We even use some of your finance stuff.

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:33.950
It's interesting for us.

00:23:33.950 --> 00:23:36.550
What would you do if you had more
resources?

00:23:36.550 --> 00:23:38.260
And I, I kinda made the same pitch.

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:41.488
And they all nodded and and then you know,
I kind of assumed and by

00:23:41.488 --> 00:23:42.879
this point I had spoken to many

00:23:42.879 --> 00:23:45.760
foundations and I'd become fairly cynical
about things.

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:47.530
I was like, okay, this isn't gonna work
out.

