00:27:03.504 --> 00:27:04.875
These things are always [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:04.875 --> 00:27:07.775
[LAUGH].

00:27:07.775 --> 00:27:11.879
&gt;&gt; I think he found me charming [UNKNOWN].

00:27:11.879 --> 00:27:15.568
But, you know, long story short he, he
said, oh wow, this makes sense.

00:27:15.568 --> 00:27:17.469
I'm a big fan, what would you do, I, I

00:27:17.469 --> 00:27:19.972
give, we make a virtual school, and all
the rest.

00:27:19.972 --> 00:27:22.301
And he's like no, this, this is great and
he nodded.

00:27:22.301 --> 00:27:24.830
And when he nodded five people in the back
of the room took notes.

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:25.411
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:27:25.411 --> 00:27:27.450
&gt;&gt; And I was like, make sure you got that.

00:27:27.450 --> 00:27:29.806
And, and so it looked like things were
going good

00:27:29.806 --> 00:27:33.085
and, and right around that same time
another meeting with Google.

00:27:33.085 --> 00:27:35.555
Google said, well what would you do with
$2 million.

00:27:35.555 --> 00:27:37.510
And I said, you know, is this an open
question,

00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:40.156
because, you know, I've got needs, you
know, I could [LAUGH].

00:27:40.156 --> 00:27:42.739
There's not a, they clarified it.

00:27:42.739 --> 00:27:44.459
[LAUGH].

00:27:44.459 --> 00:27:46.152
And, and, and, and, and so, I kind of, I,

00:27:46.152 --> 00:27:47.920
I, I, I kind of made the same, the same
idea.

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:50.943
And so, October 2010 all of these things
converged.

00:27:50.943 --> 00:27:56.207
The Gates Foundation funded us to really
get office space, hire up our core team.

00:27:56.207 --> 00:27:59.755
Google funded us, translated into ten
languages, build out the

00:27:59.755 --> 00:28:02.800
core software, and, this is what we
started to build.

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:05.565
And actually some of this was already you

00:28:05.565 --> 00:28:07.851
know, the primitive version was there
before the funding

00:28:07.851 --> 00:28:10.137
happened, but now we got essentially
competent people to

00:28:10.137 --> 00:28:12.090
work on, to work on some of this stuff.

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:13.260
And this is what we call a knowledge map.

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:14.292
And it's constantly changing.

00:28:14.292 --> 00:28:16.103
There's like sixty nodes right here.

00:28:16.103 --> 00:28:18.227
It's now 300 and we're, we're exploring

00:28:18.227 --> 00:28:20.250
different ways to visualize this right
now.

00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:22.320
But the, but the idea is very simple.

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:27.140
That top, that top star there is the most
basic mathematics, level one addition.

00:28:27.140 --> 00:28:29.493
And once you get, once you've shown
proficiency

00:28:29.493 --> 00:28:31.910
in that, it moves you down this knowledge
map.

00:28:31.910 --> 00:28:33.553
And right now, the bottom end of it,

00:28:33.553 --> 00:28:36.049
you have things like calculus and, and
whatever else.

00:28:36.049 --> 00:28:37.566
And, and actually, we can see it.

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:41.120
So this is one for getting the intuition
of a derivative.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:44.550
Where you're trying to, do, where you take
the slope of a tangent line at any

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:47.039
point and you're essentially plotting out
the derivative

00:28:47.039 --> 00:28:49.420
by picking out the slope of the tangent
line.

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:50.680
And so we're trying to make that knowledge

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:52.610
of math goes as broad and deep as
possible.

00:28:52.610 --> 00:28:55.715
Our videos you saw, they go into things
like finance and macro and micro economics

00:28:55.715 --> 00:28:57.335
and things, you know, [UNKNOWN] there's a

00:28:57.335 --> 00:28:59.610
little bit of medicine and things like
that.

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:02.753
But right now our exercises are mostly
math, but we're hoping

00:29:02.753 --> 00:29:05.193
to get that as, as broad and as deep as
possible.

00:29:05.193 --> 00:29:07.346
And this right over here, is a more basic
one,

00:29:07.346 --> 00:29:09.625
and if you ever need help, the videos are
there.

00:29:09.625 --> 00:29:13.713
This is basic subtraction, literally and,
and, you know,

00:29:13.713 --> 00:29:16.414
the one thing I always emphasize, I talked
a

00:29:16.414 --> 00:29:18.604
lot about this in the TED Talk is, we

00:29:18.604 --> 00:29:21.670
did this kinda the way is common sense,
you know?

00:29:21.670 --> 00:29:23.771
If you learned, if you went to music
class, you won't

00:29:23.771 --> 00:29:26.390
[UNKNOWN] a harder piece until you do,
master an easier piece.

00:29:26.390 --> 00:29:29.781
If you're in Tae Kwon Do, you don't take
the black belt test until you're already

00:29:29.781 --> 00:29:32.940
at a, I don't know, what is it, brown
belt, or a green belt, or whatever.

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:35.580
You only progress once you're ready for
it.

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:38.300
And that's how it works in video games and
everything else.

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:41.210
But that's not how it works in the
traditional academic model.

00:29:41.210 --> 00:29:44.069
In a traditional academic model, what is
fixed is, how long

00:29:44.069 --> 00:29:47.279
you have to learn something, and when you
have to learn something.

00:29:47.279 --> 00:29:50.585
And what's variable is, how well you
actually learn that topic.

00:29:50.585 --> 00:29:52.451
And in everything else, and I would say,
natural learning.

00:29:52.451 --> 00:29:54.640
And in this, we just said, well let's do
the opposite.

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:58.119
Let's make variable when you learn it, and
how long you have to learn it.

00:29:58.119 --> 00:30:00.528
And what's fixed is a high level of
mastery.

00:30:00.528 --> 00:30:02.278
So that you can build on that foundation.

00:30:02.278 --> 00:30:03.790
I mean, the analogy I say in, in the

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:06.520
traditional academic model, and it's not
picking on anyone.

00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:10.250
It's the model that we've all been,
frankly, indoctrinated into.

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:12.980
Is, I'm building a house, I lay the
foundation.

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:14.580
I go get the inspector to come by.

00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:16.980
And the, the, the inspector says, okay.

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:20.970
Yeah, this foundation's about 80%
structurally sound and I say, great.

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:21.620
That's passing.

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:22.440
Let's build the first floor.

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.250
And then they do the first floor, oh 70%.

00:30:25.250 --> 00:30:27.430
Oh that's passing, let's build a second
floor.

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:30.428
And then when the fourth floor collapses
and takes the

00:30:30.428 --> 00:30:34.037
whole thing down with it, I blame the
fourth floor contractor.

00:30:34.037 --> 00:30:35.863
Instead of saying what what, I had these

00:30:35.863 --> 00:30:37.852
gaps this whole time, that were just
making

00:30:37.852 --> 00:30:40.271
my whole building less and less
structurally sound,

00:30:40.271 --> 00:30:43.192
and that's exactly happening frankly, with
math education.

00:30:43.192 --> 00:30:47.767
But I think probably with most forms of
education, especially

00:30:47.767 --> 00:30:52.610
things that assume some level of mastery
of more basic ideas.

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:54.270
So we,we, we started up.

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:56.720
We got our office space, and, and
interesting, you know, and as

00:30:56.720 --> 00:30:59.500
you can see, a lot of this stuff was
unplanned serendipitous events.

00:31:00.520 --> 00:31:01.850
Right when we got our office space,
actually

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:03.470
right before we got our office space, the

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:05.680
Los Altos school board, right here, Los
Altos

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:07.580
said, hey we heard your are doing some
stuff.

00:31:07.580 --> 00:31:09.280
Why don't you come meet us.

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:10.780
So we met them and they's like what would
you do.

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:14.240
If you could do anything you wanted with a
fifth grade class room?

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:17.450
And we said well we would have every
student learning at their own pace.

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:20.540
They would master concepts before moving
on.

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:22.531
And, the role of the teacher instead of
giving

00:31:22.531 --> 00:31:24.521
this one size, one pace fits all lecture,
the

00:31:24.521 --> 00:31:26.767
teacher would get da, data, would get
analytics so

00:31:26.767 --> 00:31:29.140
that they can do focused one on one
interventions.

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:30.950
Or even better they can pair up students
with each

00:31:30.950 --> 00:31:32.650
other so that they can teach each other
and get.

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:34.260
Get even deeper learning.

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:38.980
And kind of surprising, they two days
later said, sounds good.

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:40.900
Well, let's start after Thanksgiving.

00:31:40.900 --> 00:31:42.580
And so we had to hire up a little bit.

00:31:42.580 --> 00:31:47.070
And so, this right here is a dashboard
from that Los Altos pilot.

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:49.800
And we're constantly iterating and trying
to improve these things.

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:52.130
But the general model is, each column here

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:54.700
is one of those concepts on that knowledge
map.

00:31:54.700 --> 00:31:57.430
Each row here is one of the students in
the class.

00:31:57.430 --> 00:31:59.553
Green means, and, and actually there's a,
a fancier

00:31:59.553 --> 00:32:01.328
version of this now, this is a few months
ago.

00:32:01.328 --> 00:32:04.387
Green means a student's proficient in it,
blue means they're working

00:32:04.387 --> 00:32:06.903
on it, but no need to worry, and red means
they're stuck.

00:32:06.903 --> 00:32:09.133
So the model is, hey, instead of one size
fits all

00:32:09.133 --> 00:32:11.469
lecture, why don't I get that, why don't I
get one

00:32:11.469 --> 00:32:14.660
of those students who've already mastered
Exponents level 3 to tutor

00:32:14.660 --> 00:32:17.340
this student right over here who is having
trouble with it.

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:19.907
And if they, if that doesn't work, then I
as a teacher can go do a.

00:32:19.907 --> 00:32:22.920
A focused one on one intervention.

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:23.857
And, what's neat about it is, and

00:32:23.857 --> 00:32:25.990
we've, we're actually seeing this in the
classroom.

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:30.870
Is it's optimizing the, the, the most
scarce resource in the classroom.

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:33.270
Which is actually the time with other
human beings.

00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:36.700
And so now, you know, people talk about
teachers, teacher, student ratio.

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:37.680
What we think we're optimizing is.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:40.920
The, the, the student to valuable time
with the teacher ratio.

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:43.381
Or even better, the student to valuable
time with

00:32:43.381 --> 00:32:46.135
other human beings rate, ratio, because
you also have

00:32:46.135 --> 00:32:48.831
the other, the other peers that you're
learning from

00:32:48.831 --> 00:32:51.610
and actually, one of the pilots, it was
interesting.

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:54.250
The teacher spent a significant amount of
time

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:55.880
mentoring a, a subset of the fifth graders
STAs.

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:56.380
Fifth graders.

00:32:57.810 --> 00:33:00.843
STAs.

00:33:00.843 --> 00:33:02.400
And we wanted to arm them with as much
data as possible.

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:07.921
And so, it should not freeze like this.

00:33:07.921 --> 00:33:08.804
Let me see.

00:33:08.804 --> 00:33:10.550
Well, there's a very nice chart that shows
up.

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:14.290
All right let's try this again.

00:33:14.290 --> 00:33:15.170
Oh there you go.

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:17.284
So this tells us, a teacher what the
student's been working

00:33:17.284 --> 00:33:19.710
on, and actually students have access to
all of these things.

00:33:19.710 --> 00:33:22.657
And we found that the best way to teach
elementary school sti,

00:33:22.657 --> 00:33:25.742
students to read bar graphs, is to make
the bar graphs about them.

00:33:25.742 --> 00:33:26.286
[LAUGH].

00:33:26.286 --> 00:33:28.037
&gt;&gt; We are, we are all narcissist inside,
it's the

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:33.560
This tells a, a student a, parent, a
teacher and all of this is free.

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:34.840
It's all available right now.

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:39.570
You can go be, and come your cousins, or
your nephews or, or coaches right now.

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:43.160
This tells the teacher what a student's
been focused on, videos and exercises.

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:45.190
You can drill down as much as you want.

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:47.530
This gives granular data on exactly what
problem the student did when.

00:33:47.530 --> 00:33:49.730
Now we have features you can click on.

00:33:49.730 --> 00:33:51.965
You can actually see the narrative of that
problem, what the

00:33:51.965 --> 00:33:54.360
students, what choices they got wrong,
what did they get right.

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:55.680
What hints did they use?

00:33:57.620 --> 00:34:00.300
And this right here is just another
dashboard for teachers, but for us, it

00:34:00.300 --> 00:34:01.500
tells us kind of the most powerful

00:34:01.500 --> 00:34:04.410
narrative here, and something frankly we
didn't expect.

00:34:04.410 --> 00:34:07.920
Is that the horizontal access here is just
days working on the site.

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:09.626
Vertical axis is just a count of the
number

00:34:09.626 --> 00:34:11.886
of those modules that a student shows
proficiency in.

00:34:11.886 --> 00:34:15.890
So each of those lines is essentially the
progression of a student in the class.

00:34:15.890 --> 00:34:17.639
And what we're seeing in every class we're
working with.

00:34:17.639 --> 00:34:20.551
We have Los Altos and Los Altos regular
class and Los

00:34:20.551 --> 00:34:24.015
Altos remedial class and San Jose and e,
and east Palo Alto.

00:34:24.015 --> 00:34:26.166
When you start a class.

