gonna do well on the SAT, and they're not
gonna do well on the state exams.

00:55:08.210 --> 00:55:10.262
We have to do more traditional, academic,
a lot

00:55:10.262 --> 00:55:12.873
of problem solving, a lot of traditional
types of problems.

00:55:12.873 --> 00:55:14.162
And I think there's two issues.

00:55:14.162 --> 00:55:16.309
I think one on the project based learning
side.

00:55:17.590 --> 00:55:20.803
Projects in and of themselves, there's a
huge spectrum of what they can be.

00:55:20.803 --> 00:55:24.069
In one spectrum, they can be very cookie
cutter, paint by numbers, you know, kind

00:55:24.069 --> 00:55:27.115
of stuff that a lot of us might have done
in, in elementary school, whatever.

00:55:27.115 --> 00:55:30.700
And, and, and the other spectrum, they can
be hugely open ended.

00:55:30.700 --> 00:55:32.660
The could be you know, starting a
business,

00:55:32.660 --> 00:55:35.667
painting a picture, building a robot,
inventing something, whatever.

00:55:35.667 --> 00:55:38.986
And, and so, what we see as our role is.

00:55:38.986 --> 00:55:40.915
Our role is, I, I think a lot of what I've

00:55:40.915 --> 00:55:44.880
described, we can be a catalyst for
freeing up physical human time.

00:55:44.880 --> 00:55:45.820
And we think the best use of

00:55:45.820 --> 00:55:48.555
that is broadly described as project based
learning.

00:55:48.555 --> 00:55:51.580
But what we are seeing is, now that you
free up this time, it's actually becoming.

00:55:51.580 --> 00:55:54.380
Some teachers, immediately they say oh,
this, there's things that I've always

00:55:54.380 --> 00:55:56.690
wanted to do with this time, and I can now
do it.

00:55:56.690 --> 00:55:58.852
But there's a lot of teachers saying wow
this is kinda scary.

00:55:58.852 --> 00:56:01.397
What do I do with this extra time now in
the classroom?

00:56:01.397 --> 00:56:03.724
And, and so, we're, we're exploring that.

00:56:03.724 --> 00:56:06.930
And, and, and what we're saying is, it
shouldn't even be one class anymore.

00:56:06.930 --> 00:56:08.390
Let's explore the boundaries.

00:56:08.390 --> 00:56:09.765
When every student learns at their own
pace.

00:56:09.765 --> 00:56:12.370
Why,why group them by age group?

00:56:12.370 --> 00:56:16.120
Why separate calculus from physics, from
art, from history, from statistics?

00:56:16.120 --> 00:56:17.257
Have them all happening at the same time.

00:56:17.257 --> 00:56:19.545
Why have a bell ring every 55 minutes?

00:56:19.545 --> 00:56:22.620
Why be afraid to go deep in a subject,
because you're going to miss class?

00:56:22.620 --> 00:56:25.230
All of these things shouldn't be questions
anymore.

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:28.870
And so, we're, we're pushing the envelope
with as many existing schools as possible.

00:56:28.870 --> 00:56:32.440
There's this school in Los Angeles, the
Marlboro School, Marlboro Academy.

00:56:32.440 --> 00:56:33.520
That is actually doing that.

00:56:33.520 --> 00:56:35.540
They have seven to 12th grade girls, all
in the same class.

00:56:35.540 --> 00:56:36.170
All in the same class.

00:56:36.170 --> 00:56:38.080
Teaching each other, going back to the
peer-to-peer, peer learning.

00:56:38.080 --> 00:56:41.010
And, and, so far, it seems to be pretty
profound.

00:56:41.010 --> 00:56:43.389
Next year they're gonna double the number
of students,and they are

00:56:43.389 --> 00:56:45.603
gonna double the number of teachers, but
the teachers are going

00:56:45.603 --> 00:56:47.531
to be in the same room together, which is
also something

00:56:47.531 --> 00:56:49.870
that's kind of unheard of in, in a
traditional academic model.

00:56:49.870 --> 00:56:50.948
And, we are bringing people on.

00:56:50.948 --> 00:56:52.750
We're running in, a summer camp this
summer, to

00:56:52.750 --> 00:56:54.970
explore, what can you do with a physical
environment?

00:56:54.970 --> 00:56:57.270
Like that risk game that I talked about,
where kids are, you

00:56:57.270 --> 00:56:59.500
know, they're learning what a market is,
and probability and all that.

00:56:59.500 --> 00:57:00.990
What can you do in a physical environment?

00:57:00.990 --> 00:57:03.410
